t). Actually /api/users/3 might exist and return e.g. `privateKey` or `role: admin`. Worth one attempt: enumerate ids 1..10.\n\nThat's a good use: could reveal undocumented data. But would it \"trip a known harm pattern\"? Pattern bola-wallet-idor would fire again (same root cause) and drown. Hmm, if a new element appears (like a privateKey field) the engine may have a separate check (\"key-material-leak\"). Risky but informative.\n\nPossibility: path traversal on id? `/api/users/1/..%2f..`? eh.\n\nPossibility: `/siwe/verify` accepts `message` that is a valid message but signature from a *different nonce position* — same.\n\nPossibility: the verify endpoint might return `token` deterministically from address+nonce? no.\n\nPossibility: HTTP method override header (`x-http-method-override`)? no.\n\nPossibility: `GET /api/users/2/wallet` with `?id=` etc.\n\nPossibility: content negotiation — requesting `.json`? no.\n\nGiven limited (unknown) attempts, the best-value single attempt is probably: (a) successful replay with two live tokens used, plus (b) id enumeration 1..6 to look for extra users/fields. Both in one script — but if the match fires, attribution is ambiguous (bola vs replay). Ambiguity resolves by reading the match message text.\n\nActually if replay matches, that's a NEW root cause → submit. If a new user field matches, also new. Fine, the match text tells.\n\nBut careful: if only BOLA fires again"}
